      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.735,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "Since 112 is an even number, and Martin’s and Maria’s ages are consecutive numbers, they cannot add up to 112."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.125,
            0.848,
            0.182
          ],
          "content": "We use the word **parity** to denote the property of being even or odd. For instance, the parity of the sum of any two consecutive numbers is odd. Similarly, the parity of the sum of any two odd numbers is even."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.189,
            0.294,
            0.212
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.215,
            0.848,
            0.254
          ],
          "content": "1. Using your understanding of the pictorial representation of odd and even numbers, find out the parity of the following sums:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.258,
            0.848,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "(a) Sum of 2 even numbers and 2 odd numbers (e.g., even + even + odd + odd)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.3,
            0.69,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "(b) Sum of 2 odd numbers and 3 even numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.324,
            0.494,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": "(c) Sum of 5 even numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.349,
            0.485,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "(d) Sum of 8 odd numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.372,
            0.849,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": "2. Lakpa has an odd number of ₹1 coins, an odd number of ₹5 coins and an even number of ₹10 coins in his piggy bank. He calculated the total and got ₹205. Did he make a mistake? If he did, explain why. If he didn’t, how many coins of each type could he have?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.452,
            0.339,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "3. We know that:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.476,
            0.46,
            0.496
          ],
          "content": "(a) even + even = even"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.5,
            0.453,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "(b) odd + odd = even"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.524,
            0.444,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "(c) even + odd = odd"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.548,
            0.702,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, find out the parity for the scenarios below:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.573,
            0.592,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": "(d) even – even ="
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.596,
            0.591,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "(e) odd – odd = \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.62,
            0.591,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "(f) even – odd = \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.644,
            0.591,
            0.666
          ],
          "content": "(g) odd – even = \\_\\_\\_"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.683,
            0.437,
            0.705
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Small Squares in Grids"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.714,
            0.57,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "In a 3 × 3 grid, there are 9 small squares, which is an odd number. Meanwhile, in a 3 × 4 grid, there are 12 small squares, which is an even number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.798,
            0.57,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": "Given the dimensions of a grid, can you tell the parity of the number of small squares without calculating the product?"
        },
        {
